# ProcessCraft Banking: environment variables
#
# The app needs no environment variables to run. It is a static single-page app: everything runs in the
# browser. The one optional service is the feedback form (VITE_FEEDBACK_ENDPOINT below); without it the form
# opens the user's email app instead. `npm install && npm run build` works with no .env file at all.
#
# This file is the template for later. To use it: copy it to `.env.local` (git-ignored) and fill in values.
# On Vercel, set the same names under Project > Settings > Environment Variables instead.
#
# IMPORTANT: Vite puts every variable whose name starts with VITE_ into the JavaScript bundle that is sent to
# the browser, where anyone can read it. Only put PUBLIC values in VITE_ variables (a public site URL, a
# feature flag, a publishable analytics id). Real secrets (database passwords, private API keys, signing keys)
# must never be VITE_ variables: they belong on a server (for example a Vercel Function), not in this app.

# Public: the URL the app is served from, for share links or Open Graph tags (optional, unused today).
# VITE_PUBLIC_SITE_URL=https://your-project.vercel.app

# Public: turn optional features on or off (optional, unused today).
# VITE_FEATURE_FLAGS=

# Public: the feedback form's endpoint (optional). The app already uses the author's Formspree form
# (https://formspree.io/f/xoeenavz); set this to point at another one, or to an empty value for email only.
# A form endpoint is public by design (it only accepts submissions), so a VITE_ variable is fine.
# VITE_FEEDBACK_ENDPOINT=https://formspree.io/f/your_form_id

# Public: whether that form accepts file attachments (Formspree's paid plans). Leave it unset on the free plan:
# text goes straight to the form, and a message with screenshots opens the user's email app to attach them.
# Set it to true once the plan takes files, and screenshots go straight to the form too.
# VITE_FEEDBACK_FILE_UPLOADS=true
